  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 132 PM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nWe have been able to destabilize ahead of the morning line of\nconvection as a northward moving warm front has made steady\nnorthward progress. 3000 J/Kg of MLCAPE is mostly uncapped at\nthis point, and there is a narrow ribbon of enhanced DCAPE \nnosing into the Chicago area. The stronger lead shortwave is \nlargely moving through here over the next few hours, and this\nwill keep a marginal severe thunderstorm risk in place through\nmid afternoon. The main concern would be these transient\ndownburst signatures in the more mature developed line of\nstorms. \n\nAs far as the rest of the day is concerned farther south, the \nbattle will be between the arriving mid level shortwave sliding \nthrough the area versus some capping reinforcement from lower \nlevel warm advection and anvil shielding from ongoing \nconvection. Colder temperatures aloft are focused north of\nI-80. Therefore, with an uncapped environment, expect some\nisolated to widely scattered storms farther south given that\nforcing is more muted. The signal then continues for a bit of a\nlull in activity as shortwave forcing temporarily eases, and \ncapping will get reinforced via an uptick in warm air advection\n\nAfter that we will have to take a look and see where the surface \nwarm front will set up this evening. The pattern tonight has \nsome similarity to a Maddox flash flood setup, with a low \namplitude shortwave, upper jet to the north, and a substantial \nlow level jet progged. How much of mesohigh influence is a bit \nmore vague in the latest guidance. PMM/LPMM guidance (from 0z/ \n6z) had some hefty rain totals ( 4-7\" in the region). The signal\nis a little more muted in latest guidance, at least on the \nfootprint of the heavy rainfall, but the flash flood potential \nthis evening is still elevated. The warm front has moved into \nthe Chicago metro area, and it seems like that is where it will \nremain into the evening hours. Forecast soundings do still \nsuggest, in spite of some disagreements in low level jet \nstrength and orientation with respect to the warm front, a \nperiod this evening into the early overnight where slow storm \nmotions would be favored very strong lift (lower level moisture \nconvergence), even if not quite as long lived as considered \nbefore. The most concerning time frame still looks to be in the \nmid evening through early overnight across the Chicago area \nbefore the low level jet veers more to westerly. As this occurs \nthe area of concern would shift to east Central IL (Kankakee \narea) and into NW Indiana. There is still considerable \nuncertainty here on coverage, but the signs are there to \ncontinue this message tonight. \n\nThe forcing during the day on Monday is not as clear and \nconfidence on storm evolution on Monday is somewhat low. The \nwarm front will briefly shift south (or to southern portions of \nour area) during the morning hours, however the low level flow \nwill turn back around to the southwest. Any weak ripples in the \nflow aloft though could a trigger a storm or two during the \nafternoon. Deeper forcing may not arrive until the evening with\na very warm. moist and unstable air mass in place coupled with\nstrong deep layer shear. This environment would support all\nhazards initially and also eventually bowing/line structures.  \nAnother flash flood threat would be in place, especially with\nanother uptick in the low level jet Monday evening. We will have\nto watch where the warm/stationary front lays out tomorrow, with\nhigh uncertainty on that. \n\nUnfortunately the pattern looks fairly active with a warm/moist \nairmass in place and a endless stream of shortwaves moving through \nin northwest flow upon a lingering frontal boundary Tuesday and \nWednesday.  A more dynamic system will move through eastern Canada \nlater in the week, and this will help to move the frontal boundary \nfarther south. This will take the storm track with it, and also lead \nto cooler onshore flow in this system's wake Thursday and Friday. \n\nKMD",
